{
  "coreName": "Dolphin",
  "author": "the Dolphin Team",
  "url": "https://github.com/dolphin-emu/dolphin",
  "machineSetting": "machine",
  "machines": [
    {
      "id": "GC",
      "label": "GameCube",
      "when": [
        "gamecube"
      ],
      "extensions": {
        ".iso": "GC",
        ".gcm": "GC",
        ".rvz": "GC",
        ".dol": "GC",
        ".elf": "GC"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "Wii",
      "label": "Wii",
      "when": [
        "wii"
      ],
      "extensions": {
        ".iso": "Wii",
        ".rvz": "Wii"
      }
    }
  ],
  "romFile": "game",
  "deterministic": true,
  "memoryLayoutMiB": [
    256,
    16,
    64,
    256,
    2560
  ],
  "_memoryLayoutMiB_note": "sbrk, sealed, invisible, plain, mmap. The GameCube itself is small (24MB main RAM + 16MB ARAM + caches, all anonymous mmaps since the sandbox has no shared memory), but dolphin's own working set - the software rasterizer's EFB, texture and vertex machinery, DiscIO buffers - lives in the mmap arena too, which musl routes every large allocation into.",
  "video": {
    "gpuStatesSurviveTheContext": false,
    "_comment_gpuStates": "FALSE, and measured so. The renderer does rebuild its GL objects when the calls land on a context that is not the one they came from (the bridge's GL_OP_CONTEXT_ID), and since the framebuffer-binding fix that rebuild no longer crashes - but it comes back drawing something else, and does not recover: 62% of the picture differs 40 frames after the load and 99.7% after 240. What differs, exactly: of the three memory domains only System RAM ever does - ARAM and the L1 cache stay identical throughout - and within it the difference is confined to a few megabyte-sized regions in tens of thousands of runs two to eight bytes apart, which is pixel scatter between two renderings and not a structure diverging. It shrinks rather than compounds, 807 KB at the first frame to 318 KB by the 120th, and the one word outside those regions, a pointer at 0x800000d8, matches again later. So the game's own simulation looks intact and the PICTURE is what comes back wrong - the GameCube's framebuffer living in MEM1 is why RAM sees it at all. Isolated to a single line of the rebuild, m_textures_by_address.clear() inside g_texture_cache->Invalidate(): with that one clear left out and everything else intact the reopen is byte-identical. FlushEFBCopies, TMEM::InvalidateAll, the bounding box, the perf query, RecreateEFBFramebuffer and the shader recompile were each ruled out by building without them. The clear cannot simply go - those textures name a context that is gone once the process is - so what is wanted is a texture cache that survives a context change with its contents. Until then this stays false: a resumed run that renders differently would be encoded differently.",
    "_comment": "buffer CAPACITY; the live size comes from GetVideoWidth/Height per frame, which follow the VI field being scanned out (640x480 NTSC progressive, 640x287-288 as one PAL/NTSC interlaced field). The static vsync fraction is the NTSC default; the runtime GetVsyncNumerator/Denominator exports follow the machine's actual video mode, PAL included.",
    "width": 720,
    "height": 574,
    "virtualWidth": 640,
    "virtualHeight": 480,
    "vsyncNumerator": 60000,
    "vsyncDenominator": 1001,
    "getBgra": "GetVideoBgra"
  },
  "audio": {
    "_comment": "the mixer's 48kHz stereo output, drained by machine time: rate * den / num samples per field with the remainder accumulated, so a PAL field is exactly 960 pairs and nothing is ever lost or invented.",
    "rate": 48000,
    "samplesPerFrame": 16384,
    "channels": 2,
    "get": "GetAudio"
  },
  "lag": {
    "inputWasRead": "InputWasRead"
  },
